<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="apportionment-chapter">
  <title>Apportionment: Geometry, Fairness, and Representation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every few years, the United States faces a deceptively simple question:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        How should seats in the House of Representatives be distributed among the states?
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The problem sounds straightforward.
      States with larger populations should receive more seats.
      But congressional seats are indivisible: a state cannot receive <m>7.3</m> representatives.
    </p>

    <p>
      The challenge of turning ideal fractional shares into whole-number allocations is called <term>apportionment</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Although the language comes from politics, apportionment appears everywhere:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        distributing legislative seats,
      </li>

      <li>
        allocating funding,
      </li>

      <li>
        dividing resources,
      </li>

      <li>
        assigning committee members,
      </li>

      <li>
        or even sharing coffee.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>


