  <subsection xml:id="coffee-example">
  <title>Coffee for the Department</title>

  <p>
    Before discussing congressional representation, we begin with a much smaller allocation problem.
  </p>

  <p>
    Suppose the mathematics department has purchased boxes of coffee to distribute among faculty members for their classes.
    The boxes cannot be subdivided, so each professor must receive a whole number of boxes.
  </p>

  <p>
    At first glance, this sounds easy:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Give more coffee to professors teaching more students.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    But what happens when the <q>fair</q> amount is not a whole number?
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the central difficulty of every apportionment problem.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="coffee-box-example">
    <title>Boxes of Coffee</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Courtney buys <m>45</m> boxes of coffee for the mathematics department.
        The department decides to distribute the boxes proportionally according to the total number of students taught by each professor.
      </p>

      <p>
        Assume every professor drinks coffee too, so each professor counts as an additional person.
      </p>

      <table>
        <title>Faculty Teaching Loads</title>

        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Professor</cell>
            <cell>Total Students (plus professor)</cell>
          </row>

          <row><cell>Ahmed</cell><cell>35</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Bowman</cell><cell>60</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Ceniceros</cell><cell>71</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Cockburn</cell><cell>22</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Dykstra</cell><cell>38</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Gibbons</cell><cell>55</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Kantrowitz</cell><cell>73</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Kuruwita</cell><cell>45</cell></row>
          <row><cell>LeMasurier</cell><cell>28</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Tripp</cell><cell>58</cell></row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><em>Total</em></cell>
            <cell><m>485</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Determine a way to distribute the <m>45</m> boxes.
      </p>

      <p>
        What would make an allocation seem fair?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <discussion xml:id="coffee-first-thoughts">
    <title>First Attempts</title>

    <p>
      One natural idea is to divide the total number of students by the total number of boxes:
    </p>

    <me>
      \frac{485}{45}\approx 10.78.
    </me>

    <p>
      This suggests that each box of coffee should serve about <m>10.78</m> people.
    </p>

    <p>
      So Ahmed, with <m>35</m> people, should receive about
    </p>

    <me>
      \frac{35}{10.78}\approx 3.25
    </me>

    <p>
      boxes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly, Kantrowitz should receive about
    </p>

    <me>
      \frac{73}{10.78}\approx 6.77
    </me>

    <p>
      boxes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Immediately, a problem appears:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Nobody can receive <m>0.25</m> of a box.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Apportionment problems arise precisely because proportional shares are usually fractional while allocations themselves must be whole numbers.
    </p>
  </discussion>

  <definition xml:id="coffee-quotas">
    <title>Quota</title>

    <p>
      The ideal proportional share assigned to an entity is called its <term>quota</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this example, the quota for professor <m>i</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      q_i
      =
      \frac{\text{students taught by }i}{485}(45).
    </me>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="coffee-quotas-table">
    <title>Computing the Quotas</title>

    <table>
      <title>Quota Computations</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Professor</cell>
          <cell>Population</cell>
          <cell>Quota</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Ahmed</cell>
          <cell><m>35</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3.247</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Bowman</cell>
          <cell><m>60</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5.567</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Ceniceros</cell>
          <cell><m>71</m></cell>
          <cell><m>6.588</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Cockburn</cell>
          <cell><m>22</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2.041</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Dykstra</cell>
          <cell><m>38</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3.526</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Gibbons</cell>
          <cell><m>55</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5.103</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Kantrowitz</cell>
          <cell><m>73</m></cell>
          <cell><m>6.773</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Kuruwita</cell>
          <cell><m>45</m></cell>
          <cell><m>4.175</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>LeMasurier</cell>
          <cell><m>28</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2.598</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Tripp</cell>
          <cell><m>58</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5.381</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </example>

  <discussion xml:id="rounding-problem">
    <title>The Rounding Problem</title>

    <p>
      Once quotas have been computed, we still must decide how to convert them into whole numbers.
    </p>

    <p>
      A first attempt might be ordinary rounding:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        round <m>3.247</m> down to <m>3</m>,
      </li>

      <li>
        round <m>5.567</m> up to <m>6</m>,
      </li>

      <li>
        and so on.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      But if we round every quota normally, the totals become:
    </p>

    <me>
      3+6+7+2+4+5+7+4+3+5 = 46.
    </me>

    <p>
      We only have <m>45</m> boxes.
    </p>

    <p>
      So ordinary rounding fails.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is the essential mathematical difficulty of apportionment:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        local rounding decisions do not necessarily preserve the global total.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </discussion>

  <exercise xml:id="coffee-rounding-exercise">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the department instead rounded every quota down.
      </p>

      <ol marker="a">
        <li>
          <p>
            How many boxes would be distributed?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            How many boxes would remain?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Which professors seem most deserving of the remaining boxes?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="coffee-fairness-exercise">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following two allocations:
      </p>

      <me>
        (5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,0)
      </me>

      <p>
        and
      </p>

      <me>
        (4,4,4,4,4,9,4,4,4,4).
      </me>

      <p>
        Both allocations distribute exactly <m>45</m> boxes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Explain why neither allocation seems fair.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <discussion xml:id="coffee-to-congress">
    <title>From Coffee to Congress</title>

    <p>
      This coffee problem is structurally identical to congressional apportionment.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Apportionment Analogy</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Coffee Problem</cell>
          <cell>Congressional Apportionment</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Professors</cell>
          <cell>States</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Students</cell>
          <cell>Population</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Boxes of coffee</cell>
          <cell>Seats in the House</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Quota</cell>
          <cell>Ideal representation</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The only difference is scale.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the United States House of Representatives, the indivisible objects are seats in Congress instead of coffee boxes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The mathematical questions are exactly the same.
    </p>
  </discussion>
</subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="exact-quotas">
    <title>Exact Quotas</title>

    <p>
      Suppose:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        there are <m>n</m> entities,
      </li>

      <li>
        the total number of seats is <m>M</m>,
      </li>

      <li>
        the population vector is
        <me>
          \mathbf p = (p_1,p_2,\dots,p_n),
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        and the total population is
        <me>
          P = \sum_{i=1}^n p_i.
        </me>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <definition xml:id="exact-quota-definition">
      <title>Exact Quota</title>

      <p>
        The <term>exact quota</term> of entity <m>i</m> is
      </p>

      <me>
        q_i = \frac{p_i}{P}M.
      </me>

      <p>
        The vector
      </p>

      <me>
        \mathbf q = (q_1,\dots,q_n)
      </me>

      <p>
        is called the <term>quota vector</term>.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The quota vector represents the perfectly proportional allocation we would use if seats were divisible.
    </p>

    <p>
      But apportionment requires integer allocations:
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf m = (m_1,\dots,m_n)
    </me>

    <p>
      satisfying
    </p>

    <me>
      \sum_{i=1}^n m_i = M.
    </me>

    <p>
      The entire problem of apportionment is therefore:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        How should we round the quota vector to an integer allocation?
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </subsection>


  <!-- <subsection xml:id="hamilton-method">
    <title>Hamilton's Method</title>

    <definition xml:id="hamilton-definition">
      <title>Hamilton's Method</title>

      <ol>
        <li>
          Assign every entity its lower quota:
          <me>
            \lfloor q_i \rfloor.
          </me>
        </li>

        <li>
          Count how many seats remain.
        </li>

        <li>
          Distribute the remaining seats to the entities with the largest fractional remainders.
        </li>
      </ol>
