    <example xml:id="coffee-hamilton">
      <title>Hamilton's Method for Coffee</title>

      <p>
        Using the coffee data, the quota for Ahmed is
      </p>

      <me>
        q_A = \frac{35}{485}(45)\approx 3.247.
      </me>

      <p>
        The lower quota is therefore <m>3</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Repeating this process for all professors gives:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          lower quota total: <m>41</m> boxes,
        </li>

        <li>
          remaining boxes: <m>4</m>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The remaining boxes go to the four largest fractional remainders.
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection> -->

  <subsection xml:id="hamilton-method">
  <title>Hamilton's Method and the Origins of Apportionment Paradoxes</title>

  <p>
    One of the oldest apportionment procedures used in the United States is Hamilton's method, also called the <term>largest remainder method</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hamilton's method begins with a natural observation:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Every entity should first receive the whole-number part of its quota.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    The remaining seats are then distributed according to which entities were <q>closest</q> to receiving another seat.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="hamilton-definition">
    <title>Hamilton's Method</title>

    <p>
      Given quota vector
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf q=(q_1,\dots,q_n),
    </me>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method proceeds as follows:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        Assign every entity its lower quota:
        <me>
          m_i=\lfloor q_i\rfloor.
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        Compute the number of remaining seats.
      </li>

      <li>
        Distribute the remaining seats to the entities with the largest fractional remainders
        <me>
          q_i-\lfloor q_i\rfloor.
        </me>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="coffee-hamilton-example">
    <title>Hamilton's Method for Coffee</title>

    <p>
      Recall the quota computations for the coffee example.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Quota Data</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Professor</cell>
          <cell>Quota</cell>
          <cell>Lower Quota</cell>
          <cell>Fractional Part</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Ahmed</cell>
          <cell><m>3.247</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.247</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Bowman</cell>
          <cell><m>5.567</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.567</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Ceniceros</cell>
          <cell><m>6.588</m></cell>
          <cell><m>6</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.588</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Cockburn</cell>
          <cell><m>2.041</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.041</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Dykstra</cell>
          <cell><m>3.526</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.526</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Gibbons</cell>
          <cell><m>5.103</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.103</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Kantrowitz</cell>
          <cell><m>6.773</m></cell>
          <cell><m>6</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.773</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Kuruwita</cell>
          <cell><m>4.175</m></cell>
          <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.175</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>LeMasurier</cell>
          <cell><m>2.598</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.598</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Tripp</cell>
          <cell><m>5.381</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0.381</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The lower quotas sum to
    </p>

    <me>
      3+5+6+2+3+5+6+4+2+5=41.
    </me>

    <p>
      Since there are <m>45</m> boxes total, four boxes remain to distribute.
    </p>

    <p>
      The four largest fractional parts are:
    </p>

    <me>
      0.773,\quad
      0.598,\quad
      0.588,\quad
      0.567.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore the remaining boxes go to:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Kantrowitz,
      </li>

      <li>
        LeMasurier,
      </li>

      <li>
        Ceniceros,
      </li>

      <li>
        and Bowman.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method therefore produces the allocation
    </p>

    <me>
      (3,6,7,2,3,5,7,4,3,5).
    </me>
  </example>

  <discussion xml:id="hamilton-good-properties">
    <title>Why Hamilton's Method Feels Fair</title>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method has an appealing feature:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        every entity receives either its lower quota or upper quota.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      In other words, Hamilton's method always satisfies the quota rule.
    </p>

    <p>
      This makes the method feel locally fair.
      Nobody receives dramatically more or less than their proportional share.
    </p>

    <p>
      But global fairness is more subtle.
    </p>
  </discussion>

  <subsubsection xml:id="monotonicity">
    <title>Monotonicity</title>

    <p>
      One of the central ideas in modern apportionment theory is <term>monotonicity</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Informally, monotonicity means:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        improving the inputs should not make the outputs worse.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      There are several different kinds of monotonicity relevant to apportionment.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="house-monotonicity">
      <title>House Monotonicity</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method is <term>house monotone</term> if increasing the total number of seats cannot cause an entity to lose a seat.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="population-monotonicity">
      <title>Population Monotonicity</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method is <term>population monotone</term> if an entity whose population grows faster than another entity cannot lose a seat to that entity.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      These properties sound so reasonable that one might expect every fair method to satisfy them automatically.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method does not.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="alabama-paradox">
    <title>The Alabama Paradox</title>

    <p>
      The most famous failure of house monotonicity is called the <term>Alabama paradox</term>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="alabama-definition">
      <title>Alabama Paradox</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method exhibits the <term>Alabama paradox</term> if increasing the total number of seats causes an entity to lose a seat.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      This phenomenon historically occurred when Congress considered increasing the size of the House of Representatives.
      Under Hamilton's method, Alabama would actually have lost a seat when the total number of seats increased.
    </p>

    <p>
      The paradox seems absurd:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        adding more seats should not punish a state.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Yet Hamilton's method allows this to happen because increasing the total number of seats changes:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        the quotas,
      </li>

      <li>
        the fractional remainders,
      </li>

      <li>
        and therefore the ranking of those remainders.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A small global change can completely reshuffle who receives the leftover seats.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="population-paradox-section">
    <title>The Population Paradox</title>

    <definition xml:id="population-paradox-definition">
      <title>Population Paradox</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method exhibits the <term>population paradox</term> if one entity grows faster than another entity but loses a seat to it anyway.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      This paradox violates population monotonicity.
    </p>

    <p>
      Intuitively:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        growing faster should never hurt your representation.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      But Hamilton's method can produce exactly this behavior because representation depends not only on quotas themselves, but also on the ordering of fractional remainders.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="new-states-paradox">
    <title>The New States Paradox</title>

    <definition xml:id="new-states-definition">
      <title>New States Paradox</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method exhibits the <term>new states paradox</term> if adding a new entity together with its fair share of seats changes the allocations of previously existing entities.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Historically, this paradox appeared when Oklahoma entered the United States.
    </p>

    <p>
      Even though new seats were added for Oklahoma itself, some existing states still gained or lost seats.
    </p>

    <p>
      Again, the issue comes from global remainder comparisons:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        changing the overall system changes the ranking structure of the remainders.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </subsubsection>

  <discussion xml:id="why-paradoxes-matter">
    <title>Why These Paradoxes Matter</title>

    <p>
      These paradoxes reveal something important:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        apportionment is not merely about rounding numbers.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Apportionment methods create complicated global interactions between quotas.
    </p>

    <p>
      Tiny changes in:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        total seats,
      </li>

      <li>
        populations,
      </li>

      <li>
        or the addition of new entities
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      can move an allocation from one combinatorial regime into another.
    </p>

    <p>
      To understand why this happens, it helps to stop thinking of quota vectors as lists of numbers and start thinking of them as points inside a geometric space.
    </p>

    <p>
      That geometric viewpoint leads naturally to simplices and barycentric coordinates.
    </p>
  </discussion>
</subsection>


  <!-- <subsection xml:id="paradoxes">
    <title>When Fairness Gets Strange</title>

    <p>
      Apportionment methods can behave in surprisingly counterintuitive ways.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="alabama-paradox">
      <title>Alabama Paradox</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method exhibits the <term>Alabama paradox</term> if increasing the total number of seats causes an entity to lose a seat.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="population-paradox">
      <title>Population Paradox</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method exhibits the <term>population paradox</term> if one entity grows faster than another but loses a seat to it anyway.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="quota-rule">
      <title>The Quota Rule</title>

      <p>
        An apportionment method satisfies the <term>quota rule</term> if every entity receives either its lower quota or upper quota.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      These paradoxes suggest that apportionment is not merely arithmetic.
      There is deeper geometry hiding underneath the problem.
    </p>
  </subsection>-->


  <subsection xml:id="simplex-geometry">
    <title>The Geometry of Quotas</title>

    <p>
      Notice that every quota vector satisfies
    </p>

    <me>
      q_1+q_2+\cdots+q_n=M.
    </me>

    <p>
      For example, with three states and <m>M=5</m> seats,
      every quota vector lies on the plane
    </p>

    <me>
      q_1+q_2+q_3=5.
    </me>

    <p>
      Together with the conditions
    </p>

    <me>
      q_i\ge0,
    </me>

    <p>
      this forms a geometric object called a simplex.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="quota-simplex">
      <title>The Quota Simplex</title>

      <p>
        The <term>quota simplex</term> is
      </p>

      <me>
        \mathcal Q_M
        =
        \left\{
        (q_1,\dots,q_n)\in\mathbb R_{\ge0}^n :
        \sum_{i=1}^n q_i=M
        \right\}.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Every point in the simplex represents a possible exact quota vector.
    </p>

    <p>
      The integer allocations are the lattice points inside the simplex.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="barycentric-coordinates">
    <title>Barycentric Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      Coordinates inside a simplex are called <term>barycentric coordinates</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In a triangle, barycentric coordinates measure how far a point lies toward each vertex.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="barycentric-example">
      <p>
        The point
      </p>

      <me>
        (2,2,1)
      </me>

      <p>
        lies in the simplex
      </p>

      <me>
        q_1+q_2+q_3=5.
      </me>

      <p>
        Since the coordinates sum to <m>5</m>, the point represents a valid quota vector for an apportionment problem with five seats.
      </p>
    </example>

    <p>
      The simplex perspective turns apportionment into geometry:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        apportionment methods become rules for partitioning simplices into regions.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-functions">
    <title>Apportionment Functions</title>

    <definition xml:id="apportionment-function-definition">
      <title>Apportionment Function</title>

      <p>
        Let
      </p>

      <me>
        \mathcal M_M
        =
        \left\{
        (m_1,\dots,m_n)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^n :
        \sum_{i=1}^n m_i=M
        \right\}.
      </me>

      <p>
        An <term>apportionment function</term> is a function
      </p>

      <me>
        F:\mathcal Q_M\to\mathcal M_M
      </me>

      <p>
        assigning each quota vector a unique integer allocation.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Geometrically, an apportionment function partitions the simplex into regions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every point in a region maps to the same integer allocation.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="hamilton-geometry">
    <title>Hamilton's Method Geometrically</title>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method partitions the simplex using:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        integer slab boundaries,
      </li>

      <li>
        remainder comparisons,
      </li>

      <li>
        and tie hyperplanes.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For example, the condition
    </p>

    <me>
      \lfloor q_1\rfloor=3
      \qquad\text{and}\qquad
      \lfloor q_2\rfloor=5
    </me>

    <p>
      becomes
    </p>

    <me>
      3\le q_1&lt;4,
      \qquad
      5\le q_2&lt;6.
    </me>

    <p>
      These inequalities carve the simplex into rectangular slabs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Additional inequalities comparing fractional parts divide the slabs further into polyhedral regions.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="divisor-methods">
    <title>Divisor Methods</title>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method uses fractional remainders.
      Divisor methods instead modify the divisor until rounding produces exactly the correct number of seats.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Common Divisor Methods</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Method</cell>
          <cell>Rounding Rule</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Jefferson</cell>
          <cell>Always round down</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Adams</cell>
          <cell>Always round up</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Webster</cell>
          <cell>Round normally</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Huntington–Hill</cell>
          <cell>Round using geometric means</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Each divisor method produces a different partition of the simplex.
    </p>

    <p>
      Different methods therefore encode different notions of fairness.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="optimization-methods">
    <title>Optimization and Fairness</title>

    <p>
      Another way to think about apportionment is through optimization.
    </p>

    <p>
      Instead of asking:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <q>How should we round?</q>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      we ask:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <q>What notion of unfairness are we trying to minimize?</q>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <definition xml:id="error-function">
      <title>Error Function</title>

      <p>
        An <term>error function</term> assigns a cost to an allocation:
      </p>

      <me>
        \psi(\mathbf m)
        =
        \sum_{i=1}^n
        \varphi_i(m_i,q_i).
      </me>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Different methods minimize different kinds of error:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        absolute error,
      </li>

      <li>
        squared error,
      </li>

      <li>
        relative representation error,
      </li>

      <li>
        or bias toward large or small states.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      From this perspective, apportionment methods become optimization algorithms on the simplex.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="balinski-young">
    <title>Impossibility</title>

    <p>
      Unfortunately, no apportionment method is perfect.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="balinski-young">
      <title>Balinski–Young Impossibility Theorem</title>

      <p>
        No apportionment method can simultaneously satisfy:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          the quota rule,
        </li>

        <li>
          and house monotonicity
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        for all possible apportionment problems.
      </p>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      In other words:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        every notion of fairness forces tradeoffs.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The geometry of the simplex helps us understand exactly where those tradeoffs occur.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>